/**

@page documentation_tarch_visualisation_probing-data Probing data files

probe.py reads out the unknowns at one point of a Peano patch file over
time and writes them into a csv file. It is the counterpart of render.py
(@ref documentation_tarch_visualisation_offline-conversion): it accepts the
same meta files and snapshot options, but instead of whole VTU files, it
yields one time series. The script does not rely on ParaView/pvpython or
vtk.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 src/tarch/plotter/api/probe.py --point 0.3 0.6 0.1 -o probe.csv solution.peano-patch-file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The csv file holds one row per snapshot, sorted by time. The first column
is the snapshot's time stamp, followed by all unknowns of all data sets
within the file. If the description of a data set lists one name per
unknown, e.g. `description h, hu`, the columns carry these names. ExaHyPE's
solvers write such descriptions if you give their variables names. If two
data sets use the same name, the columns are prefixed with the data set,
e.g. `SWEQ_h`. Otherwise, the columns are named after the data set and the
index of the unknown, e.g.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
time,solution_0,solution_1
0.0,1.0,0.25
0.1,1.02,0.24
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

@ref tutorials_exahype2_41_postprocessing "The postprocessing tutorial" shows the probe with named unknowns.

The most important options are

- **--point x y [z]** The probe point. For 2d data, any z coordinate is
  ignored.
- **-o/--output** Name of the csv file (default probe.csv).
- **--set-identifier** Probe only one data set from the patch file.
- **-s** The input file is a single snapshot rather than a meta file. Its
  time stamp is then 0.
- **-start/-end** Restrict the probing to a range of snapshots.
- **-j** Number of worker processes probing the snapshots in parallel.

## Which value do we pick?

There is no interpolation. Among all patches that contain the point, we
take the finest one, i.e. coarser levels of an adaptive mesh are ignored if
they are dumped, too. If the point sits exactly on a face between two
patches, we take the patch to the right/top/front. Within the patch, we
take

- for cell data, the value of the (sub)cell that contains the point;
- for vertex data, the value of the dof which is nearest to the point.
  This takes the mapping of the patch file into account, i.e. works for
  non-equidistant dof layouts such as Gauss-Lobatto points, too.

Cell data with a non-default mapping is not supported. If no patch in a
snapshot contains the point, the script writes NaN and issues a warning.

## Performance

The script parses only the offset and size of each patch and skips the
values of all patches that do not contain the point. It therefore is
considerably cheaper than a conversion into VTU. The I/O remains the same
though, i.e. each snapshot file is still read once.

 */
